The RISC OS port of Python 3.14.3
=================================

This file describes how CPython 3.14.3 was ported to RISC OS: what was
changed in the CPython sources and why, the UnixLib problems that were
worked around, and what is known not to work yet.  See BUILDING for how to
build it and ReleaseNotes for the user-facing documentation.

The port is on the riscos-314 branch of github.com/c-jo/cpython, on top of
the v3.14.3 release (python-314 branch).  It follows on from the earlier
3.8 / 3.10 ports on the riscos branch, which were built with the GCCSDK 4
toolchain.  RISC OS code in the shared sources is under "#ifdef RISCOS"
(defined in RISCOS/pyconfig.h when __riscos__ is defined), or tests
sys.platform == 'riscos' / os.name == 'riscos' in Python code.


1. Toolchain and runtime
------------------------

- Cross compiled on Linux with the GCC 16 RISC OS toolchain
  (arm-riscos-gnueabihf-gcc, https://github.com/robheaton/riscos-gcc16).
- The result is an ELF executable using the shared UnixLib
  (SharedLibs:lib.armeabihf.libunixlib/so/5), so the SharedLibs-C-armeabihf
  package is needed at run time.
- Extension modules are ELF shared objects loaded with dlopen() (libdl from
  the toolchain).  They are named <module>/cpython-314-arm-riscos/so, or are
  typed PytnExtn (&A75).
- The executable needs a WimpSlot of at least 8M ("Wimpslot not big enough
  to run ELF program" otherwise).  !Run uses -wimpslot 8M.
- Programs/python.c sets __riscosify_control to __RISCOSIFY_NO_PROCESS |
  __RISCOSIFY_NO_SUFFIX | __RISCOSIFY_NO_REVERSE_SUFFIX, so UnixLib passes
  RISC OS filenames through unchanged.  Python works with native RISC OS
  paths throughout ('.' separator, '/' before a suffix).  The variable is
  exported with default visibility so the shared UnixLib can see it.


2. Build system
---------------

configure cannot be run for RISC OS, so these files are hand maintained
(see BUILDING section 4 for regenerating them for a new Python version):

  RISCOS/pyconfig.h     generated from a cross-compile configure run by
                        RISCOS/mkpyconfig.py.  Features UnixLib has but
                        which are untested or broken are turned off (link,
                        symlink, sigaction, poll, setuid & co, fsync, ...);
                        HAVE_BROKEN_MBSTOWCS is set; off_t is 32 bits.
  RISCOS/Makefile       generated by RISCOS/mkmakefile.py.  Uses
                        RISCOS/getpath.o instead of Modules/getpath.o and
                        builds the "riscos" and "swi" modules in.
  RISCOS/config.c       the built-in module table.
  RISCOS/config.site    configure cache values for the cross-compile run.

A build Python of exactly the same version (3.14) is needed to freeze
modules and run the code generators.  Note that the frozen modules
(importlib._bootstrap_external, os, site, ...) are rebuilt from Lib, so
changes to those files need a rebuild of the executable, not just a copy of
the Lib file.


3. Path configuration (RISCOS/getpath.c)
----------------------------------------

Since 3.11 the path calculation is done by Modules/getpath.py, frozen and
run by Modules/getpath.c.  That is replaced by RISCOS/getpath.c, which
implements _PyConfig_InitPathConfig() in C (a port of the 3.8 RISC OS
getpath.c to the new PyConfig interface).  The search is described in the
comment at the top of that file.  In summary:

- program_name defaults to argv[0], or "python3" (getpath.py used to do
  this; without it startup failed with "calculate_executable: path
  configuration: no program name").
- The executable is argv[0], canonicalised with OS_FSControl 37.  UnixLib
  canonicalises argv[0] against Run$Path itself, but a bare name is also
  looked up as Run:<name>.
- prefix is found from PYTHONHOME (prefix[,exec_prefix]), then the build
  directory (<dir>.Lib containing os), then by searching upwards for
  python314.lib.os, then the PREFIX macro (<Python3$Dir>).
- exec_prefix is found the same way, using pybuilddir/txt in the build
  directory, or python314.lib.lib-dynload.
- Module files are recognised by filetype (Python &A73, PytnCode &A74) or a
  /py or /pyc suffix.
- If prefix or exec_prefix cannot be found, the usual warnings are printed,
  followed by "Searched from <dir>" giving the directory the search
  started from.
- sys.path is $PYTHONPATH, python314/zip (if present), the PYTHONPATH macro
  entries, the standard library, then lib-dynload.  DELIM is ','.


4. Changes to the C sources
---------------------------

Include/osdefs.h
    SEP is '.', DELIM is ','.

Include/pyerrors.h, Include/cpython/pyerrors.h, Objects/exceptions.c
    New built-in exception RISCOSError(errmsg, errnum), a subclass of
    Exception, used for RISC OS errors returned by SWIs.

Include/internal/pycore_fileutils.h
    _Py_stat_struct is a RISC OS specific struct: UnixLib's struct stat
    members plus st_loadaddr, st_execaddr, st_objtype and st_filetype.

Modules/posixmodule.c
    - The module is named "riscos" (os imports from it as it does from
      posix or nt).
    - stat()/lstat() use OS_File 17 (riscos_stat) and fill in the RISC OS
      catalogue information; times come from the 5-byte RISC OS timestamp
      in the load/exec addresses.  lstat() is stat() (no symlinks).
      Directories have st_filetype &1000, applications &2000.
    - os.stat_result gains st_loadaddr, st_execaddr, st_objtype and
      st_filetype.
    - scandir() uses OS_GBPB 10, so the stat information comes with each
      entry without another SWI per file.
    - listdir()/scandir() default to "@" (the CSD) rather than ".".
    - New functions os.get_filetype(path) and os.set_filetype(path, type).
    - Not setting fds non-inheritable after open() and pipe().

Python/fileutils.c
    - _Py_fstat_noraise() converts UnixLib's struct stat (catalogue
      information is not available from an fd, so st_filetype is -1).
    - encode_current_locale() works around broken wcstombs(NULL, ...)
      (HAVE_BROKEN_MBSTOWCS).
    - set_inheritable() uses fcntl() rather than ioctl(FIOCLEX) (see
      section 6).
    - _Py_open() and Py_fopen() do not set fds non-inheritable.

Python/initconfig.c
    - config_wstr_to_int() avoids wcstol(), which is unreliable in UnixLib.
    - The locale and filesystem encodings are latin-1 (unless UTF-8 mode is
      on for the locale encoding).
    - -x may be repeated to skip that many lines at the start of a script
      (Modules/main.c handles the skipping).

Python/pathconfig.c
    - sys.path[0] drops a trailing '.' separator as it does '/' on Unix.
    - Uses wcslen() rather than wcschr(p, L'\0') (see section 6).

Python/pytime.c
    The monotonic clock uses Timer_Value (TimerMod module, microseconds) if
    available, otherwise OS_ReadMonotonicTime (centiseconds).

Modules/timemodule.c
    time.sleep() busy-waits on OS_ReadMonotonicTime, rounding up to the
    next centisecond.

Parser/myreadline.c
    Interactive input uses OS_ReadLine32, so the RISC OS line editing keys
    work.  Escape raises KeyboardInterrupt if a SIGINT is pending,
    otherwise it is treated as EOF.

Python/dynload_shlib.c
    Extension suffixes are /so and /cpython-314-arm-riscos/so.  The path is
    converted to a Unix path with __unixify() before dlopen().

Modules/_io/fileio.c
    If open() fails and the name contains '.', it is retried with the last
    '.' changed to '/' (for names such as "file.txt" from other
    platforms).  A copy of the name is used, as it may be the caller's
    bytes object.  Not setting fds non-inheritable.

Modules/_posixsubprocess.c, Modules/socketmodule.c
    Not setting fds (non-)inheritable.  socket: AI_DEFAULT is redefined as
    AI_V4MAPPED, as UnixLib defines it using AI_ADDRCONFIG, which it does
    not define.

Modules/fcntlmodule.c
    UnixLib's ioctl() is not variadic, so the integer argument is passed as
    a pointer.

Modules/mmapmodule.c
    Defines my_getallocationgranularity for this configuration.

Modules/getbuildinfo.c
    The build identifier is "riscos-1".

Python/sysmodule.c
    O_NOFOLLOW is optional (UnixLib does not have it).

Programs/python.c
    __riscosify_control (see section 1).

RISCOS/swimodule.c
    The swi module: swi.swi(), swi.block and friends for calling SWIs from
    Python.  Version 2.01 for 3.14: block is initialised with PyType_Ready(),
    blocks are freed when deleted, and slicing works again.


5. Changes to the Python library
--------------------------------

Lib/riscospath.py (new)
    os.path for RISC OS: sep '.', extsep '/', pardir '^', curdir '',
    pathsep ',', devnull 'null:'.  Understands filing systems, special
    fields, disc names and the special directories ($ & % \ @).  Adds
    os.path.canonicalise() (OS_FSControl 37).

Lib/os.py
    - Imports from the riscos module and uses riscospath as os.path.
    - os.environ is built from all system variables (OS_ReadVarVal), and
      putenv()/unsetenv() use OS_SetVarVal.
    - os.read_catalogue_info() and os.write_catalogue_info() (OS_File).

Lib/importlib/_bootstrap_external.py (frozen)
    - Path separator '.', and RISC OS rules for absolute paths.
    - Source and bytecode files are found by filetype as well as by
      /py and /pyc suffix: a typed Python file "spam" is the source for
      module spam.  FileFinder caches filetypes from scandir().
    - Bytecode for a typed source file is written without a suffix and
      typed PytnCode (&A74): __pycache__.spam/cpython-314.
    - Typed PytnExtn (&A75) files are extension modules, and a typed
      __init__ file in a directory makes it a package.
    - Temporary files for atomic writes use '-' rather than '.'.

Lib/site.py (frozen)
    - site-packages are <PythonSite$Dir>.Python314.Site-Packages and
      <PythonUser$Dir>.Python314.Site-Packages; the user site is enabled if
      PythonUser$Dir is set.
    - Uses os.extsep for .pth files and LICENSE.txt.
    - Does not use pyrepl's readline (see below).

Lib/_colorize.py, Lib/_pyrepl/main.py
    Task windows do not understand ANSI/VT100 escape sequences, so colour
    output (tracebacks, help, argparse, ...) is off by default and the new
    pyrepl REPL is not used; the basic REPL (OS_ReadLine32) is used
    instead, without the "can't use pyrepl" warning.  Colour can be turned
    on with PYTHON_COLORS=1 or FORCE_COLOR, for example when connected
    from a VT100 terminal.


6. UnixLib problems found
-------------------------

These are bugs or limitations in UnixLib (GCC 16 toolchain) which the port
works around.  They may be worth reporting upstream, and are worth
suspecting when something odd happens:

- wcschr(s, L'\0') returns NULL instead of a pointer to the terminator.
  This caused a crash in _PyPathConfig_UpdateGlobal() during startup
  (reported by UnixLib as "Fatal signal received: EMT trap").
- wcstol() is unreliable.
- wcstombs(NULL, s, 0) does not work (HAVE_BROKEN_MBSTOWCS).
- ioctl() on anything other than a socket or tty fails with EINVAL, not
  ENOTTY, for unsupported requests, but FIOCLEX is defined.  CPython only
  falls back to fcntl() on ENOTTY/EACCES, so os.dup() (and anything else
  using _Py_set_inheritable) raised "OSError: [Errno 22] Invalid argument".
  fcntl(F_GETFD/F_SETFD) works, and is now used instead.
- ioctl() is not variadic.
- No F_DUPFD_CLOEXEC, O_NOFOLLOW or AI_ADDRCONFIG (but AI_DEFAULT uses
  AI_ADDRCONFIG).
- <sys/resource.h> is incomplete, so the resource module is not built.

Useful when debugging crashes: UnixLib prints a backtrace of lr values.
Addresses in the executable can be looked up with

    arm-riscos-gnueabihf-addr2line -f -i -e python <address>

(the executable is linked at &8000, so addresses are used as printed), and
the offsets shown for libunixlib/so with the same command against
<toolchain>/arm-riscos-gnueabihf/lib/libunixlib.so.5.0.0.  The UnixLib
source is in the GCCSDK repository (gcc4/recipe/files/gcc/libunixlib).


7. Packaging
------------

RISCOS/_Python3, _PythonSite and _PythonUser are the !Python3, !PythonSite
and !PythonUser application directories (',xxx' suffixes give the
filetypes).  !Python3 sets Python3$Dir, the Python3/PytnCode/PytnExtn
filetypes and Alias$@RunType_a73, and runs !PathAdd to put the executable
on Run$Path.  RISCOS/RiscPkg-* are the PackMan package control files for
the packages Python-314 (RiscPkg-314), Python-Core (RiscPkg-Core, the !Python3
application), PythonSite and PythonUser.

Installed layout:

    !Python3.Bin.python314              the executable
    !Python3.Python314.Lib              the standard library
    !Python3.Python314.Lib.lib-dynload  shared extension modules


8. Status and known issues
--------------------------

As of 3.14.3-1 the interpreter starts, finds its library from the build
tree, and runs the interactive prompt in a task window.

- Much of the standard library is untested on RISC OS.  The test suite has
  not been run.
- time.sleep() busy-waits rather than yielding to other tasks.
- File descriptor inheritance is not supported: fds are never marked
  non-inheritable.
- off_t is 32 bits, so files are limited to 2GB.
- Modules not built because their libraries are missing, or disabled, are
  listed in the table below.  resource is disabled (see section 4).
- In a task window Ctrl-D is not EOF (it is read as the character U+0004);
  use exit().

Extension modules not built on RISC OS.  "Missing" means the library or
header was not found when configure was run for the cross build; "disabled"
means the module's enable test failed (for _sqlite3, sqlite3.h was not
found).  Library names are those that configure.ac looks for.  The package
column is the RiscPkg package in RISCOS/RiscPkg-314/Control, if there is one
(Depends or Recommends).

    Module(s)                   Library                     Package
    --------------------------  --------------------------  ----------------
    zlib                        zlib                        ZLib1g
    _ssl, _hashlib              OpenSSL (libssl, libcrypto) LibSSL
    _ctypes, _ctypes_test       libffi                      LibFFI6 (Depends)
    _sqlite3                    sqlite3.h, libsqlite3 >=    LibSQLite3
                                3.15.2
    _bz2                        libbz2                      LibBZ2-1.0
    _lzma                       liblzma                     LibLZMA5
    _zstd                       libzstd                     none
    _uuid                       libuuid                     none
    _gdbm                       libgdbm                     none
    _dbm                        ndbm or gdbm compat         none
    readline                    libreadline                 none
    _curses, _curses_panel      ncurses (wide), panel       none
    _tkinter                    Tcl/Tk                      none
    resource                    <sys/resource.h>            disabled by hand

_ctypes is listed as a Depends of Python-314 but is not built, so the
libffi used at configure time needs checking.  _scproxy is macOS only and
is not expected to build.  The modules with "none" have no RiscPkg package
yet, so they would need one as well as a build.
